findings.body:
With the inactive section gone, the tabs are in section 0. `scrollToTab` still builds its `IndexPath` with `section: 1`:

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/Tabs/Views/TabDisplayView.swift" lines="170-176"}

`collectionView.isValid(indexPath:)` checks `0..<numberOfSections ~= indexPath.section`, so with one section the guard returns early every time. Scroll-to-selected-tab and scroll-to-tab silently do nothing. The XCUI tests were changed from `_1_x` to `_0_x`, which confirms the tabs are in section 0 now. The fix is to use section 0, or to build the index from the single section.

In `createTabScrollBehavior`, the fallback used when no tab is selected (for example after switching between normal and private panels) changed from `state.tabs.count - 1` to `state.tabs.count`:

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/Tabs/State/TabsPanelState.swift" lines="138-145"}

The comment still says "scroll to the bottom", and the last valid index is `count - 1`. With `count`, the index is one past the end, so the `isValid` guard drops it and the list doesn't scroll. The existing unit test `testCreateTabScrollBehavior_forScrollToSelectedTab_noSelectedTab_returnsLastTab_ifTabsNotEmpty` still expects `tabCount - 1`, so it will fail. The change looks accidental, probably from a mechanical edit while removing `isInactiveTabSection`.

The `TabsTray.InactiveTabs` strings are moved into a new `struct v147` at the end of the `String` extension, declared without `public` unlike its siblings. Nothing in the repo references these strings after this PR (grep finds no usages outside `Strings.swift`). They are now dead code, and harder to find.

If the keys are kept for localization reasons, add a comment saying so. Otherwise delete them with the feature.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Shared/Strings.swift" lines="8458-8470"}
